- What story are you telling with your new graphic?The purpose of this visualization is to examine how reported measles cases have changed over time across the six World Health Organization (WHO) regions. Rather than presenting a single global total, the graphic separates the data by region to show differences in the timing, magnitude, and direction of changes in reported cases.A regional time-series chart is appropriate because the data contain observations across both time and geographic categories. The visualization allows readers to compare regional trends, identify years with substantial increases or decreases, and consider whether changes occurred across multiple regions or were concentrated in particular regions.After reviewing the completed plot, I will identify the most important observed patterns and use them to establish the central takeaway. In particular, I will examine which regions report the highest case counts, whether major peaks occur at similar times, and whether any regions show sustained increases or decreases. These observations will form the basis of the final interpretation.I used the tidyverse package to import and prepare the measles dataset. The original regional codes were converted into more descriptive labels: Africa, Americas, Eastern Mediterranean, Europe, South-East Asia, and Western Pacific. This transformation makes the visualization more accessible to readers who may not recognize the WHO abbreviations.I then grouped the data by year and region and calculated the total reported cases for each group. This step produces a consistent structure for plotting annual case counts over time. I also excluded observations with missing values in the variables required for the visualization.An important consideration is that reported cases are not necessarily equivalent to the actual number of measles infections. Differences in surveillance systems, reporting completeness, and testing capacity can affect the observed counts. Therefore, the visualization describes reported cases rather than claiming to measure every infection worldwide.

- How did you apply the principles of CRAP?I applied the principles of Contrast, Repetition, Alignment, and Proximity (CRAP) to improve the graphic's readability and organization.Contrast. The colored lines distinguish the six WHO regions. A dark, bold title establishes the subject of the graphic, while muted gridlines provide reference points without competing with the data. The visual emphasis remains on the trends rather than on decorative elements.Repetition. The same line style, axis formatting, and typography are used consistently throughout the plot. Each region is represented by a line, and the legend maintains the same colors used in the chart. This repetition helps readers learn the visual encoding quickly and apply it consistently when comparing regions. Alignment. The chart uses a common horizontal axis for years and a common vertical axis for case counts. This shared coordinate system allows readers to compare changes across regions without adjusting to different scales. The labels and title are positioned consistently, and the legend is centered below the chart.Proximity. The title and subtitle appear together above the plot, clearly establishing the subject and measurement. The legend is placed near the chart so readers can readily connect each regional label with its corresponding line. The source note is separated from the main data display because it provides supporting information rather than the primary message.

- How did you apply Kieran Healy's principles of great visualizations or Alberto Cairo's five qualities of great visualizations? The design also reflects principles discussed by Kieran Healy and Alberto Cairo.First, the visualization emphasizes the data rather than decorative elements. A restrained background, limited gridlines, and consistent typography help readers focus on the observations. The chart uses a line graph because connecting annual values is appropriate for displaying change over time.Second, the graphic supports comparison. Every region shares the same axes, so differences in case counts and changes over time can be evaluated directly. The numerical vertical axis also makes the magnitude of differences visible rather than relying exclusively on visual impressions.Third, the design aims for clarity and accessibility. Descriptive regional labels replace abbreviations, the axes identify the time variable and the outcome being measured, and the legend explains the color encoding. The caption identifies the intended data source. These elements help readers understand the graphic without needing to consult the analysis code.Cairo's emphasis on truthfulness and functional design is particularly relevant. The visualization should represent the observations faithfully, avoid misleading scales, and communicate uncertainty or data limitations where necessary. The chart therefore uses a common scale and does not imply that reported case counts fully capture the underlying incidence of measles.The graphic should remain useful for exploration. Readers can examine the overall direction of each regional trend, identify unusual peaks, and compare the timing of changes. The figure is designed to support these comparisons without forcing a conclusion that is not supported by the data.

This project uses a regional time-series chart to explore how reported measles cases have changed over time. The design combines a straightforward visual encoding with consistent formatting, descriptive labels, and a common scale to make comparisons easier.

The CRAP principles help organize the figure, while the principles associated with Healy and Cairo encourage clarity, accuracy, accessibility, and meaningful interpretation. Together, these considerations support a visualization that is not only visually organized but also useful for understanding the data.

